===Noun===
{{en-noun|skull and crossbones|skulls and crossbones}}

# A [[depiction]] of a [[human#Adjective|human]] [[skull]] and [[two]] [[crossed#Adjective|crossed]] [[femur]]s ([[thighbone]]s), a [[symbol#Noun|symbol]] of [[death]] [[traditionally]] [[use#Verb|used]] on the [[Jolly Roger]], but now as a [[warning#Noun|warning]] of [[toxicity]] or other [[danger#Noun|dangers]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard C. Colton|author2=Edmund S. Ward|author3=Charles H. Wager|title=Practical Handbook of Industrial Traffic Management|edition=5th|location=Washington|publisher=Traffic Service Corporation|year=1973|page=579|oclc=714024|passage=When poisons are involved, red '''skull and crossbones''' are added.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William G. Boltz|title=The Origin and Early Development of the Chinese Writing System|location=New Haven, Conn.|publisher={{w|American Oriental Society}}|year=1994|page=16|isbn=978-0-940490-78-9|passage=The '''skull and crossbones''' is, to be sure, a visual sign that communicates a very specific meaning.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Michael Flynn|authorlink=Michael Flynn (writer)|chapter=Friends in High Places|editor=[[w:David G. Hartwell|David G[eddes] Hartwell]]|title=Rogue Star|series=[[w:Tor Books|A Tor Book]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Tom Doherty|Tom Doherty Associates]]|month=April|year=1998|page=144|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/roguestar00flyn/page/144/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-312-86136-0|passage=Because when I saw him again, the Bird had two '''skull-and-crossbones''' stenciled on his X-ray camera.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=The Regiment de Tren: Transportation under Fire|editors={{w|Cary Nelson}} and Jefferson Hendricks|title=Madrid 1937: Letters of the Abraham Lincoln Brigade from the Spanish Civil War|location=Abingdon, Oxfordshire; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Routledge}}|year=1996|year_published=2003|page=554|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=3yHKAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA554|isbn=978-0-415-91408-6|passage=On the floor was a big flag whose significance I could not make out. There was a big red cross in the center and around it were two '''skull and crossbones''' and two other insignia with crossed torches, crossed scythes, and a smaller cross in the center.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jason Corburn|title=Street Science: Community Knowledge and Environmental Health Justice|series=Urban and Industrial Environments|location=Cambridge, Mass.|publisher={{w|MIT Press}}|year=2005|page=182|isbn=978-0-262-03333-6|passage='''Skulls and crossbones'''&nbsp;- were used to label toxic storage sites.}}

